WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.179 --> 00:00:16.480
this is SARS Co-V - it belongs to the
family of coronaviruses named for the

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:22.650
crown like spikes on their surfaces
SARS Co V - can cause Covid 19 a

00:00:22.650 --> 00:00:28.510
contagious viral infection that attacks
primarily your throat and lungs what

00:00:28.510 --> 00:00:34.570
actually happens in your body when you
contract the corona virus what exactly

00:00:34.570 --> 00:00:43.060
causes your body to develop pneumonia
and how would a vaccine work the corona

00:00:43.060 --> 00:00:48.010
virus must infect living cells in order
to reproduce let's have a closer look

00:00:48.010 --> 00:00:53.410
inside the virus genetic material
contains the information to make more

00:00:53.410 --> 00:00:59.020
copies of itself a protein shell
provides a hard protective enclosure for

00:00:59.020 --> 00:01:03.360
the genetic material as the virus
travels between the people it infects an

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:08.950
outer envelope allows the virus to
infect cells by merging with the cell's

00:01:08.950 --> 00:01:14.070
outer membrane projecting from the
envelope are spikes of protein molecules

00:01:14.070 --> 00:01:20.140
both a typical influenza virus and the
new corona virus use their spikes like a

00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:25.270
key to get inside a cell in your body
where it takes over the cells internal

00:01:25.270 --> 00:01:30.400
machinery repurposing it to build the
components of new viruses when an

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:36.250
infected person talks coughs or sneezes
droplets carrying the virus may land in

00:01:36.250 --> 00:01:42.100
your mouth or nose and then move into
your lungs once inside your body the

00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:49.030
virus comes in contact with cells in
your throat nose or lungs one spike on

00:01:49.030 --> 00:01:53.320
the virus inserts into a receptor
molecule on your healthy cell membrane

00:01:53.320 --> 00:01:59.460
like a key in a lock this action allows
the virus to get inside your cell a

00:01:59.460 --> 00:02:05.080
typical flu virus would travel inside a
sac made from your cell membrane to your

00:02:05.080 --> 00:02:08.630
cell's nucleus that houses all its
genetic material

00:02:08.630 --> 00:02:12.140
the coronavirus on the other hand
doesn't need to enter the host cell

00:02:12.140 --> 00:02:18.500
nucleus it can directly access parts of
the host cell called ribosomes ribosomes

00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:23.150
use genetic information from the virus
to make viral proteins such as the

00:02:23.150 --> 00:02:27.650
spikes on the virus's surface a
packaging structure in your cell then

00:02:27.650 --> 00:02:32.390
carries the spikes in vesicles which
merge with your cells outer layer the

00:02:32.390 --> 00:02:37.250
cell membrane all the parts needed to
create a new virus gathered just beneath

00:02:37.250 --> 00:02:42.230
your cell's membrane then a new virus
begins to butt off from the cell's

00:02:42.230 --> 00:02:47.960
membrane now with the virus spreading in
your body how can you develop pneumonia

00:02:47.960 --> 00:02:52.220
symptoms for this we'll have to look
into your lungs

00:02:52.220 --> 00:02:58.220
each lung has separate sections called
lobes normally as you breathe air moves

00:02:58.220 --> 00:03:02.540
freely through your trachea or windpipe
then through large tubes called

00:03:02.540 --> 00:03:08.600
bronchioles called
bronchioles and finally into tiny sacs

00:03:08.600 --> 00:03:15.020
called alveoli your Airways and alveoli
are flexible and springy when you

00:03:15.020 --> 00:03:20.480
breathe in each air sac inflates like a
small balloon and when you exhale the

00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:26.750
sacs deflate small blood vessels called
capillaries surround your alveoli oxygen

00:03:26.750 --> 00:03:31.400
from the air you breathe passes into
your capillaries and then carbon dioxide

00:03:31.400 --> 00:03:35.480
from your body passes out of your
capillaries into your alveoli so that

00:03:35.480 --> 00:03:41.090
your lungs can get rid of it when you
exhale your Airways catch most germs in

00:03:41.090 --> 00:03:45.620
the mucus that lines your trachea
bronchi and bronchioles in a healthy

00:03:45.620 --> 00:03:50.630
body hair like cilia lining the tubes
constantly push the mucus and germs out

00:03:50.630 --> 00:03:55.490
of your Airways where you might expel
them by coughing normally cells of your

00:03:55.490 --> 00:03:59.840
immune system attack viruses and germs
that make it past your mucus and cilia

00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:04.550
and enter your alveoli however if your
immune system is weakened like in the

00:04:04.550 --> 00:04:09.200
case of a corona virus infection the
virus can overwhelm your immune cells

00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:14.330
and your bronchioles and alveoli become
inflamed as your immune system attacks

00:04:14.330 --> 00:04:18.979
the multiplying viruses the inflammation
can cause your alveoli to fill with

00:04:18.979 --> 00:04:23.540
fluid making it difficult for your body
to get the oxygen it needs you could

00:04:23.540 --> 00:04:28.280
develop low burn pneumonia where one
lobe of your lungs is affected or you

00:04:28.280 --> 00:04:33.290
could have bronchopneumonia that affects
many areas of both lungs pneumonia may

00:04:33.290 --> 00:04:40.100
cause difficulty breathing chest pain
coughing fever and chills confusion

00:04:40.100 --> 00:04:46.630
headache muscle pain and fatigue it can
also lead to more serious complications

00:04:46.630 --> 00:04:51.500
respiratory failure occurs when your
breathing becomes so difficult that you

00:04:51.500 --> 00:04:56.000
need a machine called a ventilator to
help you breathe these are the machines

00:04:56.000 --> 00:04:59.990
that save lives and that medical device
companies currently ramp up production

00:04:59.990 --> 00:05:04.250
for whether you would develop these
symptoms depends on a lot of factors

00:05:04.250 --> 00:05:10.040
such as your a agent whether you already
have an existing condition while this

00:05:10.040 --> 00:05:14.990
all sounds scary the push to develop a
corona virus vaccine is moving at high

00:05:14.990 --> 00:05:19.670
speed studies of other corona viruses
led most researchers to assume that

00:05:19.670 --> 00:05:24.770
people who have recovered from a SARS
co2 infection could be protected from

00:05:24.770 --> 00:05:29.480
reinfection for a period of time but
that assumption needs to be backed by

00:05:29.480 --> 00:05:34.550
empirical evidence and some studies
suggest otherwise there are several

00:05:34.550 --> 00:05:38.720
different approaches for a potential
vaccine against the corona virus the

00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:43.130
basic idea is that you would get a shot
that contains faint versions of the

00:05:43.130 --> 00:05:48.230
virus the vaccine would expose your body
to a version of the virus that is too

00:05:48.230 --> 00:05:53.470
weak to cause infection but just strong
enough to stimulate an immune response

00:05:53.470 --> 00:05:58.160
within a few weeks cells in your immune
system would make markers called

00:05:58.160 --> 00:06:03.620
antibodies which would be specific for
only the corona virus or specifically

00:06:03.620 --> 00:06:08.780
its spike protein antibodies then attach
to the virus and prevent it from

00:06:08.780 --> 00:06:13.970
attaching to your cells your immune
system then responds to signals from the

00:06:13.970 --> 00:06:19.520
antibodies by consuming and destroying
the clumps of viruses if you then catch

00:06:19.520 --> 00:06:24.170
the real virus at a later stage your
body would recognize it and destroy it

00:06:24.170 --> 00:06:29.989
in other words your immune system is now
collecting evidence on whether this will

00:06:29.989 --> 00:06:35.479
be possible safe and effective is part
of what's taking researchers so long to

00:06:35.479 --> 00:06:40.249
develop a vaccine it's a race against
time to develop a vaccine amid a

00:06:40.249 --> 00:06:44.989
pandemic each step in vaccine
development usually takes months if not

00:06:44.989 --> 00:06:50.269
years an Ebola vaccine broke records by
being ready in five years

00:06:50.269 --> 00:06:55.489
the hope here is to develop one for the
new coronavirus in a record-breaking 12

00:06:55.489 --> 00:07:02.329
to 18 months while all this will take
time stay home if you can to protect the

00:07:02.329 --> 00:07:07.399
most vulnerable and don't forget to wash
your hands for at least 20 seconds and

00:07:07.399 --> 00:07:14.749
as often as possible this video was a
